g in the output—between each value there are 2 spaces, which suggests gdf might be enforcing a minimum width or padding rule for readability. Looking at the actual spacing patterns, I see that gdf uses 2-space separators in human-readable mode but only 1 space in the default numeric output—likely because the column widths are driven by minimum padding values in the source code rather than just the data itself. Checking if the column widths are 5, 5, 5, and 4 for Size, Used, Avail, and Use% respectively, which would explain the spacing pattern in the header output. So the spacing works out perfectly—with w1 = 5, there are exactly 6 spaces between \"Filesystem\" and \"Size\", and the data rows follow the same pattern with 2 spaces between the device name and size values.\n\nThis reveals that gdf enforces a minimum column width of 5 for numeric columns in human-readable mode, even when the natural width would be smaller. The Size and Used columns both get padded to 5 characters, while Avail naturally fits at 5 from its header, and Use% stays at its natural width of 4. Hmm, Use% is 4, not 5. So it's not ALL numeric columns. Maybe the minimum of 5 only applies to size-related columns (Size, Used, Avail)?\n\nOr maybe the minimum is just 5 for columns that display sizes. In GNU df source, there might be something like setting a field width to the maximum of the current width and 5 for block/size fields.\n\nLet me check by looking at the default mode too — all the sizes are at least 7+ digits so the minimum of 5 doesn't matter. It only really shows up in human-readable mode where sizes are short like \"927G\" which is just 4 characters. I'm wondering if gdf uses a fixed format string that always allocates at least 5 characters for the size columns and 4 for the percentage, which would explain the pattern I'm seeing. The minimum width for block-related columns might be hardcoded separately from the header width. So the minimum width of 5 only applies to the block/size column